<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
    <TrimMode>full</TrimMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The second trim canary that runs (#3965). Quartz.Trimming.Canary references Quartz and the three
    packages a scheduler loads in its own process; this one references the three that put a scheduler on a
    wire, and runs every end of every wire in one process over one Kestrel:

      - Quartz.AspNetCore serves a scheduler over the HTTP API, and Quartz.HttpClient drives it over a
        loopback socket, then reads its events off the same API's server-sent event stream;
      - Quartz.Dashboard.Agent dials a second scheduler out to a SignalR hub on that Kestrel, which speaks
        the dashboard's half of the agent protocol with the contract's own records (AgentCanaryHub), and
        answers what the hub asks of it down the tunnel.

    What it proves that a compile cannot: the request delegates the generator wrote are the ones that run,
    the wire contract reads and writes with reflection-based System.Text.Json switched off on both sides, a
    job named as a string in a request body comes back out as a type that runs, and the SignalR client's
    On<T>, InvokeAsync<T> and client-to-server streaming bind the hub payloads through the contract's
    generated metadata.

    Signed, unlike Quartz.Trimming.Canary, because Quartz grants this project InternalsVisibleTo: the hub
    half of the agent protocol lives in Quartz.Dashboard, which Blazor keeps untrimmable, so the canary
    writes a hub of its own and has to spell the protocol's records exactly as the agent does. A strong-named
    assembly can only name a friend by public key.

    Everything else is Quartz.Trimming.Canary's, for the reasons its csproj gives. No TrimmerRootAssembly,
    no suppressions file applied, and warnings that do not stop the link: build/Build.cs reads what the
    publish reported and checks it against the ILLink.Suppressions.xml of Quartz, Quartz.AspNetCore,
    Quartz.HttpClient and Quartz.Dashboard.Agent.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>false</ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <IlcTreatWarningsAsErrors>false</IlcTreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      SignalR's one feature switch for a native AOT server, which no SDK sets on its own: a Web SDK project
      spells it as the SignalRCustomAwaitableSupport property, and this console-SDK host names the runtime
      option directly. With it on - the default - the server builds each hub method's executor by
      reflecting over the return type's awaiter, and natively that reflection misses a plain Task: the hub
      method is taken for synchronous, its Task is returned unawaited, and the dispatcher completes the
      method's client-to-server streams before the first item arrives. That is how the agent's event stream
      came up the tunnel to nobody on this canary's first native run, while the same publish, trimmed and
      JIT-compiled, read every event. Off, the server recognises Task, Task<T>, ValueTask and ValueTask<T>
      by type, which is the path SignalR's own native AOT support takes.
    -->
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Hub.IsCustomAwaitableSupported" Value="false" Trim="true" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz.AspNetCore\Quartz.AspNetCore.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz.Dashboard.Agent\Quartz.Dashboard.Agent.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz.HttpClient\Quartz.HttpClient.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
